    <h2 id="hypotheses-overview">Hypotheses at a glance</h2>
    <p>The page tests fifteen hypotheses, H1 to H15, listed here in full before any of them is discussed. Each is stated with its metric and thresholds in one table in <a href="#hypotheses">Section 8</a>, followed by the falsifiers RJ-F1 to RJ-F6.</p>
    <ol class="list-none pl-0">
      <li>H1. Form space is much smaller than source space for the same programs. (Supported on 185 hand-written programs: median rho 9.34.)</li>
      <li>H2. The checker is cheap relative to generation. (Supported on Apple M5 Max in <code>reported_j</code>: <code>C_check / E_model = 0.000582 &lt;= 0.05</code>, <code>1,718×</code> cheaper.)</li>
      <li>H3. Prediction in representation space reaches useful acceptance.</li>
      <li>H4. Energy per accepted program falls.</li>
      <li>H5. Energy ranking concentrates mass on valid Forms. (Contrastive <code>FormEBM-v2</code> reaches AUROC <code>0.8922 &gt;= 0.85</code> and <code>84.05%</code> solvable top-1 accuracy, and span-normalized <code>FormEBM-v3</code> raises unseen-shape AUROC to <code>0.9437 &gt;= 0.85</code> with <code>83.44%</code> defect-head and <code>80.98%</code> composite solvable top-1 accuracy vs <code>0.618</code> for uncalibrated <code>FormJEPA-v1</code>; zero-shot OOD H15 pooled AUROC rises from <code>0.2585</code> to <code>0.5278</code>, while cross-module signature/trace calibration beyond <code>0.5278</code> remains open.)</li>
      <li>H6. The reference apps are encodable as Forms.</li>
      <li>H7. Security does not depend on the model. (Fuzzed arm measured: 0 violations in 1,275 mutants.)</li>
      <li>H8. Every accepted Form replays deterministically. (Supported on 10,500 streams across six targets: 0 mismatches.)</li>
      <li>H9. Cost is predicted before execution.</li>
      <li>H10. Learning on the quotient (canonical Forms) saves training data.</li>
      <li>H11. Energy is monotone in edit distance. (Contrastive <code>FormEBM-v2</code> reaches <code>93.0% &gt;= 80%</code> strict 5-step monotonicity, and decomposed <code>FormEBM-v3</code> reaches <code>94.0%</code> on <code>E_depth</code> at Spearman <code>0.9966</code> and <code>92.0%</code> on <code>E_total</code> at Spearman <code>0.9846</code> across 100 unseen cumulative defect chains vs <code>36.0%</code> for <code>FormJEPA-v1</code>; unconstrained same-line random-walk slices remain below <code>80%</code>.)</li>
      <li>H12. The decision head is calibrated on gate outcomes.</li>
      <li>H13. A discrete latent plans better than a continuous one over long edit sequences.</li>
      <li>H14. A public deterministic ladder of exact proposers closes a measurable share of a registered task set with zero model calls, and that share is the baseline the learned proposer must beat on the residual. (Supported: 111 of 151 tasks, 73.5%, leaving 40 open tasks.)</li>
      <li>H15. Proposer classes are compared on the residual of 40 tasks under a matched budget, alongside Gear-0 crystallization and the 3-Gear Cascade. Benchmarked across six single-shot cloud/local proposers (<code>17/40</code> to <code>34/40</code>), purpose-trained <code>FormJEPA-v1</code> (<code>22/40</code> at <code>k = 1</code>, <code>27/40</code> at <code>k = 16</code>; <code>25/40</code> typed <code>k = 1</code> with <code>FormEBM-v2</code> reranking; <code>24/40</code> typed <code>k = 1</code> and <code>27/40</code> multi-proposer <code>k = 1</code> under <code>FormEBM-v3</code> <code>E_def</code>), bounded checker-feedback <code>H15-Iterative</code> with public standard-library headers (<code>40/40</code> on <code>Opus 5.5</code> and <code>Sonnet 5.5</code> with <code>0</code> contradictions, <code>39/40</code> on <code>Grok 4.20</code>), and Shape D (<code>DNULLMM1</code>) Gear-0 replay (<code>40/40</code> at <code>0</code> calls; <code>16.67×</code> 3-Gear episode reduction and <code>200/200</code> admissions on <code>Zipf(0.85)</code>); diffusion and evolutionary arms remain open.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="falsifiers-overview">Falsifiers at a glance</h2>
    <p>Six observations that would defeat part of the premise, stated in full in Section 8 after the hypotheses they test.</p>
    <ol class="list-none pl-0">
      <li>RJ-F1. Lift coverage plateaus below 80% on any of the five target languages.</li>
      <li>RJ-F2. Writing the intent specifications for the reference applications costs more than writing their Forms by hand.</li>
      <li>RJ-F3. H3 fails while the token arm passes at the same gate.</li>
      <li>RJ-F4. H11 fails and search does not reach accepted Forms within the beam budget.</li>
      <li>RJ-F5. The decision head's calibration never beats a frequency table over shape hashes.</li>
      <li>RJ-F6. A trace-trained token-space model closes the residual of 40 tasks at equal energy and equal gate acceptance, with no use of the Form quotient.</li>
    </ol>

