    <p>The deterministic ladder in the proposer section runs before any of these (H14: <code>111/151</code> closed at <code>0</code> calls, leaving <code>40</code> residual tasks). Section 8 reports both the six-model off-the-shelf H15 baseline plus Shape D (<code>DNULLMM1</code>) Gear-0 crystallization and the purpose-trained <code>FormJEPA-v1</code> arm plus 3-Gear Cascade on those 40 tasks, while keeping the diffusion and evolutionary arms, intent-to-Form H3, and wall-meter server joule parity explicitly open.</p>

    <h2 id="7-world-of-code">7. A World of Code</h2>
    <p><strong>Definition.</strong> A World of Code is the state space of Forms, plus the exact transition and the exact acceptance rule. In the notation of the thesis, the state space is <span class="not-prose">S</span>, the transition is the function δ, and acceptance is the checker's verdict on a Form. "World model", for code, means a model of this space. Unlike a physical world model, the physics is known exactly. Part 4 of the thesis says that for a JEPA-style predictor. The same physics bounds every proposer in Section 6. CWM learns an interpreter from traces. V-JEPA 2 learns physics from video. In a Form, the transition is given. There is no irreducible noise in the next state.</p>
    <p><strong>Where learning is removed, and where it remains.</strong> Canonicalization, equivalence and replay are computations. Once the Laws of Coding fix them, a model does not have to learn them. What remains to learn is the map from a stated intent to a Form, guidance of the search, and ranking of whatever the deterministic ladder leaves open (<code>40</code> real-history tasks after H14 run 3). Once any proposer solves one of those residual tasks and passes the gate, Gear-0 crystallization turns every recurrence (and <code>7/9</code> alpha-renamings under <code>rce fmt nameless</code>) into a <code>0.325 µs</code> deterministic lookup.</p>
    <p><strong>Evaluation.</strong> A test suite is not the Laws of Coding. PatchDiff reports that 7.8% of patches counted correct fail developer tests, and that resolve rates are inflated by about 6.4 points. The METR trial reports 16 developers, 246 tasks, and a measured completion time 19% higher with early-2025 tools. SWE-Bench Pro, on the board fetched 6 November 2026, still uses fail-to-pass and pass-to-pass tests as the oracle. Multi-SWE-bench widens the set of languages to Java, TypeScript, JavaScript, Go, Rust, C and C++. Acceptance on this page is the gate (<code>rce check</code> exit <code>0</code> plus obligation preservation), verified further against historical originals by <code>rce hash</code> and <code>rce equiv</code>.</p>
    <p><strong>Falsifier of the widened claim.</strong> RJ-F6, stated with the others in Section 8, names the observation that would defeat the Form quotient as a help to proposers: if a token-space model closes the residual of 40 tasks at equal energy and equal gate acceptance without the Form quotient. Single-shot frontier token models close up to <code>34/40</code> (<code>85.0%</code>) on first encounter, whereas <code>FormJEPA-v1</code> (<code>8.42M</code> parameters over quotient-normalized Form S-expressions) matches local 35B token-space repair at <code>27/40</code> (<code>k = 16</code>) at over an order of magnitude lower <code>reported_j</code> and <code>rce fmt nameless</code> supplies <code>7/9</code> zero-call quotient hits under alpha-renaming; controlled equal-size quotient-versus-surface sample-efficiency tests (H10) and intent-to-Form H3 remain open.</p>

    <h2 id="8-one-concrete-instance-the-reality-code-engine">8. One concrete instance: the Reality Code Engine</h2>
    <p>RCE is one closed program form among possible others; the questions above apply to any language with a small, checkable program shape. In RCE every program is a Form P = (S, E, δ, D, λ):</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>S, state:</strong> the typed data the program holds, for example the cursor position and text buffer of an editor.</li>
      <li><strong>E, events:</strong> the inputs it can receive, for example "key pressed" or "file opened".</li>
      <li><strong>δ (delta), transition function:</strong> the rule giving the next state from the current state and an event.</li>
      <li><strong>D, derivations:</strong> the recorded, hash-chained history of steps, which makes every run replayable bit for bit.</li>
      <li><strong>λ (lambda), output:</strong> what the program shows or emits for a given state, for example the visible lines of text.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A checker accepts a Form or names the rule it breaks. At commit <code>f87d6af2</code> (23 November 2026; Rust checker binary unchanged from <code>1762fb4b</code>), the public reference checker replays an accepted Form exactly, passes <code>2,221</code> of <code>2,236</code> conformance tests with <code>0</code> failures (<code>837</code> Rust unit tests), emits Rust, Python, JavaScript and Scala, and is backed by <code>5,393</code> Lean 4.36.0 kernel theorem constants from <code>1,264</code> source declarations across <code>64</code> modules (theorems T1 to T63, verified inside Linux <code>bubblewrap</code> across all six independent proof checkers of <code>lake check --paranoid</code>). On Apple M5 Max, dual-channel <code>reported_j</code> receipts record <code>49.04 mJ</code> per Form check (L3 <code>ri_energy_nj</code>, <code>7.32 ms</code>/Form) and <code>15.44 J</code> per full 62-Form check, trace-run and five-target emit suite (<code>2.32 s</code>/pass; <code>measured_j = None</code>, <code>board_metered = false</code>). The full description is on the <a href="/about/rce/">RCE topic page</a> and at <a href="https://github.com/openIE-dev/rce">github.com/openIE-dev/rce</a>.</p>
    <p><strong>RCE-JEPA</strong> is the working name, kept so the thread stays findable. The subject is World Coding. JEPA, a joint embedding predictor, is the premise: predict meaning, not surface text. The proposer slot is any learned proposer over Forms. Checking, proofs, derivation, emission and placement stay with RCE. The aim is the best performance per clock cycle and per joule. <em>Working hypothesis:</em> if all code can be encoded in the Form, all code can be produced by joint embedding or energy-based models over it. The hypotheses below are designed so that the evidence can refute this.</p>

    <h3>Defined quantities</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Output space.</strong> Free-form generation over vocabulary V with length L has raw space |V|<sup>L</sup>. Ω<sub>n</sub> (omega) is the set of valid Forms of size n (Form nodes); Ω<sub>n</sub><sup>✓</sup> ⊆ Ω<sub>n</sub> is the subset the checker accepts. Reported as log<sub>2</sub>|Ω<sub>n</sub>| bits, estimated by grammar counting and sampling.</li>
      <li><strong>Description length.</strong> b(P) is the bits to encode Form P canonically; b(code) = tokens × log<sub>2</sub>|V| for the equivalent emitted source. Ratio ρ (rho) = b(code) / b(P).</li>
      <li><strong>Verification cost.</strong> C<sub>check</sub>(P): wall time, cycles and joules for the checker to decide P. Unit tests give C<sub>test</sub>; a passing test is evidence, not proof.</li>
      <li><strong>Acceptance probability.</strong> a = P(checker accepts and task specification satisfied | intent), measured as pass@1 over the benchmark.</li>
      <li><strong>Joules per accepted program.</strong> J<sub>acc</sub> = (E<sub>model</sub> + E<sub>check</sub> + E<sub>retry</sub>) / N<sub>accepted</sub>, summed over a benchmark run (labelled <code>reported_j</code> for L2/L3 counters and <code>measured_j</code> only when an external hardware power meter is attached).</li>
      <li><strong>Cycles per accepted program.</strong> K<sub>acc</sub>, the same in CPU (central processing unit) or GPU cycles where counters are readable.</li>
      <li><strong>AUROC</strong> (area under the receiver operating characteristic curve): the probability that a randomly chosen accepted Form gets a lower energy than a randomly chosen one that requires change; 0.5 is chance, 1.0 is perfect.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Argument (opinion, to be tested).</strong> A Form has five typed slots with fixed composition rules. Constraining output to Ω<sub>n</sub> removes most of |V|<sup>L</sup> before the model is consulted, so fewer bits are predicted per program (ρ &gt; 1), the checker gives a definite verdict, accepted or requires change, in place of a probabilistic test, and a requires-change verdict is cheap and structured, naming the rule, so retries are guided rather than blind.</p>
    <h3 id="hypotheses">Falsifiable hypotheses H1 to H15</h3>
    <p>All fifteen hypotheses in numerical order. H1 to H7 test the RCE-JEPA thesis directly, H8 to H10 test the "Laws of Coding" thesis above, H11 to H13 test the proposer, H14 measures the deterministic ladder beneath it, and H15 compares proposer classes on the 40-task residual alongside Shape D (<code>DNULLMM1</code>) Gear-0 crystallization.</p>
